(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-blocks-ventricular-arrhythmias-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Arrhythmias II: Blocks and Ventriculars Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 72-year-old man with congestive heart failure and an ejection fraction of 20% is in stable, sustained monomorphic ventricular tachycardia with a pulse. Which intravenous antiarrhythmic should be avoided?","choices":["Amiodarone","Magnesium","Procainamide","Lidocaine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Procainamide should be avoided in a prolonged QT or congestive heart failure; lidocaine or amiodarone are the other named options for stable monomorphic ventricular tachycardia. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"An 81-year-old man takes no medications and has never had a heart attack. A routine electrocardiogram shows a constant PR interval of 0.25 seconds with every beat conducted, and he asks why his heart is doing this. Which explanation is most accurate?","choices":["An overactive vagus nerve","Age-related wear of the pathway","A silent tick-borne infection","Hidden damage from a heart attack"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The most common cause of first-degree atrioventricular block is age-related degenerative change of the conduction system, so the impulse takes longer to cross the atrioventricular node. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with stable coronary artery disease, who has never had a heart attack, asks whether he is at any risk of sudden cardiac death. Which statement is accurate?","choices":["Coronary disease is the top risk","Risk starts only after an infarction","Only inherited rhythms raise risk","Risk applies only after age 80"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Coronary artery disease is the most common predisposing condition for sudden cardiac death, even in adults without a history of myocardial infarction. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 27-year-old graduate student drinks several energy drinks a day and has occasional skipped beats. A monitor shows isolated premature ventricular contractions; his echocardiogram and electrolytes are normal. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Start lifelong amiodarone","Cut caffeine and stimulants","Avoid all exercise","Avoid dairy products"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Alcohol, caffeine and stimulants are named triggers of premature ventricular contractions, and low-risk patients are reassured and helped to eliminate triggers. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man has fatigue, shortness of breath on exertion and palpitations. His electrocardiogram shows every P wave conducted to a narrow QRS with a constant PR interval of 0.34 seconds. No offending drug or reversible cause is found. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Intravenous magnesium","Permanent pacemaker","Reassurance only","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. First-degree block causes symptoms once the PR interval exceeds 0.3 seconds, and a permanent pacemaker is considered for symptomatic patients when no reversible cause is found. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man returns from a camping trip in Connecticut with fatigue and an expanding red rash around a tick bite on his thigh. His electrocardiogram shows a constant PR interval of 0.30 seconds with every P wave conducted. Which infection should be suspected as the cause of his conduction delay?","choices":["Viral pharyngitis","Lyme disease","Infective endocarditis","Chagas disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lyme disease (Lyme carditis) is a named infectious cause of atrioventricular block at every degree, and a tick bite with an expanding rash points to it. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man has a regular wide QRS tachycardia at 190 per minute with identical complexes. He is confused, his blood pressure is 72/40, and a carotid pulse is palpable. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Defibrillation","Intravenous amiodarone","Transcutaneous pacing","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Altered mental status and hypotension define instability; with a pulse still present, unstable monomorphic ventricular tachycardia is treated with synchronized cardioversion. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman taking a loop diuretic for heart failure has new palpitations felt in her neck. Telemetry shows frequent premature ventricular contractions. Which laboratory values should be checked first?","choices":["Potassium and magnesium","Thyroid hormone, cortisol","Troponin and lipase","Glucose and lactate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Electrolyte imbalances, especially hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia (with hypercalcemia), are main causes of premature ventricular contractions, and a loop diuretic can lower both. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"An 80-year-old man is found unresponsive and pulseless. The monitor shows a flat line in two leads with no P waves or QRS complexes. Compressions are underway. Which drug is a mainstay of treatment?","choices":["Magnesium","Epinephrine","Amiodarone","Lidocaine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. High-quality chest compressions and epinephrine by intravenous or intraosseous push at intervals are the mainstays of treatment for asystole. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 79-year-old woman has a week of fatigue and dizziness. Her heart rate is 36. The electrocardiogram shows regular P waves at 80 per minute and regular wide QRS complexes at 36 per minute, with no consistent relationship between them; some P waves sit inside T waves. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Complete heart block","Mobitz type II block","Mobitz type I block","Ventricular tachycardia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Two separate regular rhythms, with atria faster than the ventricles and P waves falling anywhere (even within T waves), is third-degree (complete) atrioventricular block. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man survives an out-of-hospital cardiac arrest from pulseless ventricular tachycardia. After stabilization no reversible cause is found. Which device is most appropriate before discharge?","choices":["Implantable loop recorder","Biventricular pacemaker","Implantable defibrillator","Permanent pacemaker"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Once stabilized after life-threatening ventricular tachycardia or fibrillation, most patients need an implantable cardioverter defibrillator for secondary prevention of sudden cardiac death. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has a right bundle branch block found on routine testing. He has no symptoms, and his examination is normal. Which approach to treatment is correct?","choices":["Treat any underlying condition","Permanent pacemaker","Biventricular pacemaker","Catheter ablation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Right bundle branch block is usually asymptomatic, and its treatment depends on the underlying condition; prognosis depends on the presence and type of heart disease. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman is in asystolic cardiac arrest, and resuscitation continues. Her family asks about her chances. Which statement about this rhythm is most accurate?","choices":["Better than fibrillation","Recovery is expected","Prognosis is poor","A shock should restore it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Asystole and pulseless electrical activity carry a poor prognosis and are usually end-of-life rhythms; ventricular tachycardia and fibrillation have the best prognosis. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man has fatigue and ankle swelling. A 24-hour monitor shows premature ventricular contractions in 22% of beats, and his echocardiogram shows a newly reduced ejection fraction; coronary angiography and other tests find no other cause. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Catheter ablation","Reassurance only","Digoxin","Intravenous magnesium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Isolated premature ventricular contraction-induced cardiomyopathy with heart failure signs and symptoms, in a high-risk patient with a burden over 15%, is treated with catheter ablation. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman is scheduled for an implantable cardioverter defibrillator. She asks what the device will do inside her chest. Which explanation is most accurate?","choices":["Detect and shock fatal rhythms","Slowly dissolve artery plaque","Only record her rhythm","Replace her heart medications"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The implantable cardioverter defibrillator prevents sudden cardiac death: sensing electrodes detect fatal arrhythmias and the device delivers a shock, and newer models can also pace. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with hypertension and diabetes for 20 years is found to have Mobitz type II block. He takes no rate-slowing drugs and has had no infections. He asks what caused it. Which explanation is most accurate?","choices":["Conduction system wear","An overactive vagus nerve","His blood pressure tablets","A recent viral illness"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The most common cause of Mobitz type II is acquired disease of the His-Purkinje system, especially degenerative change from old age, chronic hypertension and diabetes. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman with known pulmonary sarcoidosis faints at work. Her heart rate is 35, and the electrocardiogram shows P waves at a regular rate with no relationship to slow QRS complexes. Which statement about her block is accurate?","choices":["It is caused by vagal tone","It will resolve without treatment","It is a drug side effect","Sarcoidosis is a named cause"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Inflammatory and infiltrative disease, namely myocarditis, amyloidosis and sarcoidosis, is a named cause of complete heart block. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman is brought in after fainting at home. She is confused, her heart rate is 34 and her blood pressure is 78/40. The electrocardiogram shows regular P waves, a constant PR interval in the conducted beats and many P waves with no QRS after them. Medications given so far have not raised her rate. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Defibrillation","Observation on a monitor","Transcutaneous pacing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. When medications are not used or do not work in unstable Mobitz type II block, the emergent measure is transcutaneous pacing, which bridges her to a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman in ventricular fibrillation arrest has received shocks, epinephrine and amiodarone, and she remains in ventricular fibrillation. Which drug may be considered next?","choices":["Metoprolol","Adenosine","Lidocaine","Verapamil"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lidocaine is considered for ventricular fibrillation that remains refractory to shocks after amiodarone. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man who grew up in rural Bolivia has months of fatigue and exertional breathlessness. His heart rate is 38 and the electrocardiogram shows P waves completely dissociated from slow, wide QRS complexes. Which infection is a named cause of his conduction disease?","choices":["Tuberculosis","Chagas disease","Lyme disease","Malaria"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Chagas disease is a named cause of complete heart block; it is a parasitic infection endemic in parts of Latin America such as rural Bolivia. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man in cardiac arrest remains in ventricular fibrillation despite repeated shocks, continuous cardiopulmonary resuscitation and epinephrine. Which antiarrhythmic is given next?","choices":["Digoxin","Amiodarone","Adenosine","Diltiazem"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. If ventricular fibrillation or pulseless ventricular tachycardia persists after shocks, amiodarone is given by rapid intravenous or intraosseous push. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with a long-standing left bundle branch block now has dizziness, shortness of breath and dyspnea on exertion that resemble a heart failure flare. What does the onset of symptoms from the block usually indicate?","choices":["Accessory pathway","Severe heart disease","A harmless variant","A drug side effect"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Most patients with left bundle branch block are asymptomatic; those who develop symptoms usually have severe underlying cardiac disease and do not tolerate the block well. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man on telemetry has an 18-second run of identical wide QRS complexes at 180 per minute. During the run his blood pressure falls to 70/40 and he nearly faints; the run then stops on its own. How should this episode be classified?","choices":["Ventricular fibrillation","Ventricular bigeminy","Sustained ventricular tachycardia","Non-sustained ventricular tachycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ventricular tachycardia counts as sustained if it lasts 30 seconds or more or causes hemodynamic collapse in under 30 seconds; his hypotension makes an 18-second run sustained. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with ischemic cardiomyopathy has had a regular wide QRS tachycardia at 160 per minute, with identical complexes, for 10 minutes. She is alert and talking, her blood pressure is 118/74, and she has no chest pain or shortness of breath. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Immediate defibrillation","Synchronized cardioversion","Intravenous adenosine","Intravenous antiarrhythmic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hemodynamically stable, monomorphic sustained ventricular tachycardia is pharmacologically cardioverted with intravenous lidocaine, amiodarone or procainamide, with monitoring, intravenous access, a 12-lead electrocardiogram, oxygen and labs. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman on telemetry has torsades de pointes (QRS complexes that twist around the baseline). An intravenous push of magnesium broke the first run, but short runs keep recurring. She is alert with a pulse and a normal blood pressure. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Intravenous procainamide","Synchronized cardioversion","Magnesium infusion","Intravenous amiodarone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Torsades in a conscious patient is broken with intravenous magnesium and then treated with more magnesium as an infusion: magnesium, and more magnesium. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man on telemetry has a prolonged QT after starting a new medication and then develops polymorphic ventricular tachycardia (QRS complexes that vary in shape and twist around the baseline). He becomes unresponsive and pulseless. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Intravenous magnesium only","Transcutaneous pacing","Defibrillation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pulseless polymorphic ventricular tachycardia, including torsades, is treated as cardiac arrest with defibrillation and cardiopulmonary resuscitation; magnesium follows. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with hypertension is told that a routine electrocardiogram shows a long-standing left bundle branch block, unchanged from tracings years ago. He feels well and has a normal ejection fraction. He asks whether it needs treatment. Which answer is most accurate?","choices":["He needs a pacemaker now","He needs a defibrillator","He needs catheter ablation","It is usually left untreated"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Left bundle branch block is usually left untreated; the exception is symptomatic left bundle branch block with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, which gets a biventricular pacer. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman with five days of vomiting and diarrhea has a prolonged QT and brief runs of torsades de pointes (QRS complexes that twist around the baseline). Which electrolyte disturbances should be sought as causes of her long QT?","choices":["High potassium, high calcium","Low sodium, high chloride","High sodium, low phosphate","Low potassium, low calcium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Besides drugs, hypokalemia and hypocalcemia are named causes of acquired long QT syndrome, which underlies most torsades de pointes. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman taking metoprolol and diltiazem for palpitations has a new rhythm in which the PR interval lengthens until a QRS is dropped. She has mild fatigue. Which part of her history is a named cause of this rhythm?","choices":["Her mild fatigue","Her female sex","Her rate-slowing drugs","Her history of palpitations"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers and nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers, with digoxin, are named causes of Mobitz type I block, so her medication list is the first place to look. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman five days after hip replacement develops sudden breathlessness, pleuritic chest pain and a heart rate of 118. Her electrocardiogram shows a new right bundle branch block. Which acute cause of the block should be suspected?","choices":["Pulmonary embolism","Aortic dissection","Pericardial effusion","Hypertensive crisis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A sudden increase in right ventricular pressure, as from a pulmonary embolism, is a named cause of right bundle branch block, and her postoperative setting fits. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 31"}]};
